 ## The window
 
-oculus runs in a browser tab or in its own app. The app (`npm run app` from the checkout, or the `oculus.app` that `npm run app:package` makes) is Electron: it starts the server itself, shows the page in a window of its own with a Dock icon, and ends the server when it quits, leaving the tmux sessions running. It adds what a browser tab cannot give the page: windows that remember where they were, a floating pane that becomes a window of its own (Move into New Window in the pane's and the tab's menus, Copy into New Window in the tab's, Restore into Main Window and Switch to Main Window in a popped-out window), the keys Cmd+W, Ctrl+Tab and Ctrl+Shift+Tab, Cmd+N, Cmd+Shift+N, Cmd+Shift+W, Cmd+, (settings), Cmd+R (reload the window) and Cmd+F, a find bar, a folder sheet for Add Folder to Workspace, spelling suggestions in the text menu, and a dark window frame; a page in the app with no stored theme starts on vscode dark modern. The windows of the app share one arrangement as the browser tabs do. When the server ends on its own, a dialog quotes why and offers Restart, which starts the server again; the open windows keep their pages, their unsaved text and their running terminals, reconnect to the new server on their own and take its new write token, and a window on another port than the new server's loads its page from the new one. A link to another site opens in the default browser. Started again, the app opens its windows where they were; a second start brings the running app forward. Everything else on this page is the same in both.
